Native concurrency qualification prerequisites
=============================================

This test-only branch extends the pinned #35/Kerf executing-child packer.
It introduces no production message family or public interface. Do not include
the native_concurrency_test object or its default-off Kconfig option in a
production submission. Old six-CPU halt lanes remain unchanged.

First gate: nested CPU pool roundtrip
-----------------------------------

Enable CONFIG_MK_X86_NATIVE_CONCURRENCY_TEST=y and DEBUG_FS in an x86 GCC
build. Build with build_x86_child.py from the explicit clean source so its
mk-x86-build.json binds the successful source/tree/config/binary outputs.
Use prepare_x86_child.py's existing arguments with --profile=nested.
This uses the same pinned #35 packaging and Kerf; no package installation.

Boot ONLY the documented disposable QEMU q35 VM:

  timeout --kill-after=5s 240s qemu-system-x86_64 \
    -machine q35 -accel tcg,thread=multi -cpu max -smp 8 -m 3072 \
    -kernel "$bundle/bzImage" -initrd "$bundle/host-initrd.cpio.gz" \
    -append 'console=ttyS0,115200 rdinit=/init panic=0 kernelcore=1G log_buf_len=4M mk_test=x86-nested-host mk_native_concurrency_test=1 nokaslr' \
    -nographic -monitor none -nic none -no-reboot

Run the frozen verifier, requiring BOTH actual VM and verifier exit success:

  python3 -B "$bundle/verify_x86_nested.py" --bundle "$bundle" \
    --revision "$revision" "$serial_log"

Three physical CPUs remain with the host; three run a real 512 MiB child;
two remain free. The child pools its last secondary with a 16 MiB chunk,
returns it through a real /resources overlay, then removes every nested
chunk. Require actual online/owned sets, empty free pool, zero local park/slot
addresses, specific successful transactions, and parent confirmed halt/unload.
The root-only local_snapshot is protected by mk_overlay_mutex and reports
full-width physical IDs. It does not inject a stop or claim cached park flags
prove execution has stopped. The option AND explicit boot parameter AND known
QEMU DMI identity must be present, including in the child.

If return/teardown fails, preserve allocations and raw output; do not force
recovery to make this normal roundtrip pass. The VM can then be discarded.
No CPU pool/controller race can be qualified before this prerequisite passes.
This gate alone is NOT native worker/controller, callback, IRQ/RCU, writer,
PREEMPT_RT, DMA, crash-kexec or physical-hardware proof.

Run all test_*.py via Python unittest discovery and the existing *_test.sh
runners. test_verify_x86_nested.py exercises false-pass evidence mutations;
its fabricated trace is a verifier unit test, never native qualification.

Second gate: native process admission and timeout drain
------------------------------------------------------

Package the enabled clean build with --profile=admission. Use the same
eight-CPU QEMU command, replacing mk_test=x86-nested-host with
mk_test=x86-admission-host and increasing the outer timeout to 360 seconds.
Run the frozen verify_x86_admission.py with --bundle, --revision and raw log.
Both VM and verifier must exit zero. This first repeats the nested prerequisite.

The child holds the real controller mutex before an incoming CPU removal,
then reverses the order: an accepted CPU worker holds the mutex while a real
local pool overlay waits. Busy ACK, explicit remove retry, re-add and local
pool retirement use real resource operations, transactions and ownership
readback. The final case holds an accepted CPU add before ACK past the real
requester timeout. The parent must retain the CPU and reject active unload;
requested halt must wait for that accepted work before confirming every CPU.

Process gates are root-only debugfs files under mk-native-concurrency-test.
Each hold is bounded to 30 seconds; automatic release invalidates the run.
The fixed event buffer rejects overflow. Native sequence numbers correlate
debugfs observations, resource request IDs, physical CPUs and ACK results;
controller transaction IDs are a separate namespace. The hooks never delay
IRQ handlers. Literal oracle fixtures are not native evidence.

Remote CPU removal while a child-local park allocation still exists is NOT
covered: the controller-first case retires that allocation before retry;
the worker-first case removes its CPU before creating the allocation.
IRQ/RCU callback, MKTTY close, ring writer retirement, lockdep/RT and physical
hardware qualification remain separate gates; this profile cannot certify them.

Third gate, first case: actual IPI callback versus unregister
-----------------------------------------------------------

Use --profile=rcu, mk_test=x86-rcu-host and verify_x86_rcu.py with the same
eight-CPU QEMU/build/bundle discipline. The child sends a nonce using the
existing MK_MSG_USER family, through the real ring and x86 vector. H0 holds
the actual callback inside its RCU read section; a precreated H1 kthread
unlinks that exact handler and calls synchronize_rcu(). H2 observes the
waiter sleeping with unlink/grace-entry records present and no return/free,
then releases. A monotonic IRQ-safe two-second deadline aborts qualification
and releases automatically; this gate never sleeps or prints from IRQ context.

Preallocated records prove vector/hardirq entry, last context access, grace
period completion, actual handler/context frees, and two consumed messages
with exactly one callback invocation. The second message arrives after
unregister and must not invoke the old callback. Only a task-observer reference
is retained for the disposable VM's one-shot report; it does not pin the tested
handler/context. The test files require the existing config/boot/QEMU guards.
This does not qualify messaging terminal cleanup, MKTTY close or ring writers.

Message registry replacement and terminal cleanup
------------------------------------------------

Use --profile=registry and mk_test=x86-registry-host, with an explicit
mk_registry_mode=replace or mk_registry_mode=cleanup. The frozen verifier is
verify_x86_registry.py with --mode matching the launch. Each mode needs its
own disposable VM; terminal cleanup is not reversible and is never followed
by a messaging reinitialization.

Both cases enter the real message wrapper and callback on H0. H1 performs
the actual type-list detach and unregister/cleanup. Another H1 kthread tries
same-type registration: only its genuine failed mutex_trylock counts as
contention, followed by the normal blocking mutex_lock. H2 observes both
waiters sleeping and releases the bounded IRQ gate. Require last callback
access before native grace completion, handler/wrapper frees, and registration
return. Replacement must succeed and receive the second message; terminal
cleanup must reject registration with -ESHUTDOWN and invoke neither old nor
replacement callback on the second consumed message.

Replacement finishes with ordinary confirmed halt/unload. Terminal cleanup
leaves the instance ACTIVE with its assigned CPUs and memory retained; the
host reports survival and discards the VM. It MUST NOT be described as safe
instance reclamation or ordinary shutdown after cleanup. These tests still
do not qualify MKTTY final close, ring writers, lockdep/RT or real hardware.
